186417935} {:answer/id #uuid "7b00678a-ca27-52ad-ba5a-61632b06f63a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n\\in N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417944} {:answer/id #uuid "cd04eee5-6b3b-52a1-a4ea-dcdefef3f4cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n\\in Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b00678a-ca27-52ad-ba5a-61632b06f63a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n\\in N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417944} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4e9d017-a142-50f4-82a8-0b219e05832b" :answer-field/key "field-15" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61f38221-2cec-5289-9323-193f490aea4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-183802" :question/problem "Use mathematical induction to prove that\n${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nfor all $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-6}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-7}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}={{field-9}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-10}} & ={{field-11}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & ={{field-12}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{field-13}} \\\\  & ={{field-14}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nis true for all {{field-15}}." :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5(1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5(k+1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving matrix identities using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to factor out one instance of the matrix, as follows: \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1}={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5k+5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5(k+1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required. \n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nis true for all $n\\in N.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083177 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6befd731-de3f-545c-aca4-b51b67c68ace" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Matrix Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dc8b06e-c864-5dbf-a712-c415f35957f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\be